 food products, and automotive industries. The company was founded in 1978 and is headquartered in North Liberty, Iowa.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Michael J. Gerdin | Chairman of the Board, President & CEO | Age 55 | Compensation: $1.1M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 3K people. Revenue per employee of $222K ranks in the bottom quartile among Industrials peers. Stock-based compensation of $606 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 5\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Heartland Express, Inc. is a small-cap industrials business with a $780M market cap on $806M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -6% over the past three years and +1% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The company is burning through $67M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","Accruals are elevated at -11.9% of assets, and Sloan showed firms building earnings through accruals rather than cash systematically underperform.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin -5.0\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.1% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 51% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Operating margins 16% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},